{"name":"Cat Woman","hex":"#cddfbb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cat","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-3 language code for Catalan."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Terms relating to animals.\n  A mammal of the family Felidae.","A mammal of the family Felidae."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cat"},{"term":"woman","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An adult female human.","All female humans collectively; womankind."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To staff with female labor.","To make effeminate or womanish."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/woman"}],"entries":[{"title":"Catwoman","blocks":[{"text":"Catwoman is a character appearing in American comic books published by DC Comics. Created by Bill Finger and Bob Kane, she debuted as \"the Cat\" in Batman #1. She has become one of the superhero Batman's most prominent enemies, belonging to the collective of adversaries that make up his rogues gallery, as well as Batman's best known and most enduring love interest, with many stories depicting their complex love–hate relationship. Since 1993, Catwoman has had her own ongoing series, Catwoman."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/e4/Catwoman_Infobox.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catwoman"},{"title":"Cat","blocks":[{"text":"The cat, also called domestic cat and house cat, is a small domesticated carnivorous mammal. It is a member of Felidae, the family of mammals in the order Carnivora also colloquially referred to as cats. An obligate carnivore requiring a predominantly meat-based diet, it has retractable claws adapted to killing small prey species such as mice and rats. It has a strong, flexible body, quick reflexes, and sharp teeth, and its night vision and sense of smell are well developed. It is a social species, but a solitary hunter and a crepuscular predator.\nCat communication includes meowing, purring, trilling, hissing, growling, grunting, body language, and scent marking. It can hear sounds too faint or too high in frequency for human ears, such as those made by small mammals. It secretes and perceives pheromones. Cat intelligence is evident in its ability to adapt, learn through observation, and solve problems. The domestic cat is the only domesticated species of the family Felidae."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Siam_lilacpoint.jpg/330px-Siam_lilacpoint.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cat"},{"title":"Woman","blocks":[{"text":"A woman is an adult female human. Before adulthood, a female child or adolescent is referred to as a girl."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Woman_at_Lover%27s_Bridge_Tanjung_Sepat_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Woman_at_Lover%27s_Bridge_Tanjung_Sepat_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woman"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/a/a5/Catwoman-batman01.jpg/250px-Catwoman-batman01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/a5/Catwoman-batman01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/a/a5/Catwoman-batman01.jpg/250px-Catwoman-batman01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Selina Kyle's first appearance as the Cat in Batman #1 (spring 1940)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACatwoman-batman01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/e/ee/Catwoman_%28Jim_Balent_and_Joe_DeVito%27s_art%29.png/330px-Catwoman_%28Jim_Balent_and_Joe_DeVito%27s_art%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/e/ee/Catwoman_%28Jim_Balent_and_Joe_DeVito%27s_art%29.png/330px-Catwoman_%28Jim_Balent_and_Joe_DeVito%27s_art%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Catwoman, painted by Joe DeVito over pencil art by the titular character's series artist Jim Balent. Balent penciled Catwoman for several years and defined the visuals of the character for a long period.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACatwoman_(Jim_Balent_and_Joe_DeVito's_art).png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/71/Adam_Hughe%27s_Catwoman.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/71/Adam_Hughe%27s_Catwoman.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Catwoman (vol. 3) #48, with art by Adam Hughes. Catwoman's costume and style transitioned to a spy aesthetic in the 2000s.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAdam_Hughe's_Catwoman.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/1/1b/Catwoman%26Catgirl.jpg/250px-Catwoman%26Catgirl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/1b/Catwoman%26Catgirl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/1/1b/Catwoman%26Catgirl.jpg/250px-Catwoman%26Catgirl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Selina with her then-sidekick, Catgirl (2010), art by Tony Daniel","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACatwoman%26Catgirl.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/04/Catwoman_Various_incarnations.jpg/250px-Catwoman_Various_incarnations.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumb